WEBVTT

00:02:18.270 --> 00:02:21.700
Most exciting.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:24.100
Yes, it was exciting.

00:02:24.100 --> 00:02:29.090
I played Susan, Doctor Who's granddaughter, in the two Doctor Who movies.

00:02:31.530 --> 00:02:39.820
latest invention. What, a police box? Of course not. This is TARDIS. TARDIS? It stands for

00:02:39.820 --> 00:03:00.000
Time and Relative Dimension in Space. Oh. Go inside. Yeah? Susan was

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:06.940
supposed to be Doctor Who's right-hand man. She understood the making of the TARDIS and how it all

00:03:06.940 --> 00:03:12.980
worked and a little bit of a know-all. In electrokinetic theory, space expands to accommodate

00:03:12.980 --> 00:03:17.900
the time necessary to encompass its dimensions. My little fellow scientist, we've been working

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:23.420
on TARDIS for many years. This is the final component. You are privileged, young man,

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:30.000
to be the first visitor to our time and space machine. I had watched the TV series with William

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:35.100
Hartnell playing Doctor Who so I knew what it was about and the thought of

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:39.600
actually doing it on film I was really really thrilled. The Dalek films were

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:44.140
produced by a pair of New Yorkers Max Joseph Rosenberg and Milton Sabotsky.

00:03:44.140 --> 00:03:47.400
Sabotsky in particular was always looking over his shoulder at what his

00:03:47.400 --> 00:03:52.260
principal rivals Hammer were doing and in the early 1960s Hammer had hit upon

00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:55.820
the idea of making family movies which were specifically released at holiday

00:03:55.820 --> 00:04:00.000
time. They were making pirate films but Sabotsky wanted to cash in on

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:03.380
this trend and so he cast around and the Daleks were very big at the time, had

00:04:03.380 --> 00:04:07.080
never appeared in colour before, never appeared on the big screen, so in '64

00:04:07.080 --> 00:04:10.950
secured the rights to make the first Dalek movie.

00:04:10.950 --> 00:04:18.220
It began just as you see here. Do you know what you have just done? You have

00:04:18.220 --> 00:04:22.460
transferred us in time and space and I hadn't even set the controls. No, I don't know where we are.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.830
We could be anywhere in the universe and at any time.

00:04:25.830 --> 00:04:30.000
Yes, this is how it began. The adventure that started by

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:40.980
accident taking us out of this time and place to a lost planet come with us into that strange new

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.820
world i cannot guarantee your safety but i can promise you unimagined things

00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:28.390
They know we've escaped through the door.

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:37.810
Come with us to the petrified forest, meet the Thars, the blonde giants who have survived

00:05:37.810 --> 00:05:40.070
the monstrous rule of the Daleks.

00:05:40.070 --> 00:05:41.070
We must get to the city!

00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:42.950
They could have scanners here or anything, I'm going back.

00:05:42.950 --> 00:05:43.950
No you're not!

00:05:43.950 --> 00:05:46.040
We'll be killed, we'll never defeat the Daleks!

00:05:46.040 --> 00:05:50.760
Remember, we are watching you!

00:05:50.760 --> 00:05:54.440
We can't destroy you!

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:55.440
It's your turn!

00:05:55.440 --> 00:05:56.440
Go back!

00:05:56.440 --> 00:05:57.500
Come!

00:05:57.500 --> 00:06:00.000
These are the people.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:06.430
by the Daleks, Doctor Who, the brilliant science professor, the young man who triggered off

00:06:06.430 --> 00:06:12.470
this strange journey, the professor's frightened granddaughter, and the youngster who inherited

00:06:12.470 --> 00:06:19.890
her grandfather's adventurous spirit, Doctor Who and the Daleks.

00:06:20.550 --> 00:06:30.000
Now you can see them in color on the big screen, closer than ever before, so close you can feel

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:44.190
their fire. So thrilling, you must be there. Look behind you! Stop the countdown! Destroy the planet!

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:07.710
back to meet us!

00:07:14.030 --> 00:07:16.010
Rosenberg and Sabotsky's firm, Amicus,

00:07:16.110 --> 00:07:18.170
had never attempted anything on this scale before,

00:07:18.630 --> 00:07:20.270
and to do science fiction of this quality

00:07:20.270 --> 00:07:22.610
needed a budget bigger than they could master by themselves.

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.120
So they approached a chap called Joe Vigoda,

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:26.540
who ran a company called Aru,

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:28.300
and it was his company's name

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:30.000
which went on the credits in return.

00:07:30.000 --> 00:07:34.420
for the co-finance they required, although they are, of course, very much amicus films.

00:07:34.810 --> 00:07:38.990
The two movies were adaptations of two of the earliest Doctor Who television serials

00:07:38.990 --> 00:07:44.160
written originally by Terry Nation, and script edited by David Whittaker,

00:07:44.340 --> 00:07:46.520
who worked on the scripts for the movies as well.

00:07:46.890 --> 00:07:49.530
I went to a couple of meetings, I think, at the beginning,

00:07:50.280 --> 00:07:53.940
and again, by this time, I was absorbed into doing something else,

00:07:53.940 --> 00:08:00.000
and I asked David Whittaker to translate my screenplay,

00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:03.120
television screenplay into a screenplay for cinema.

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:07.980
The world of the films is very different to the world of the television show.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:12.100
In the television series, the lead character is simply called the Doctor,

00:08:12.420 --> 00:08:15.540
and he's a Time Lord, he's an alien, from a planet called Gallifrey.

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:20.780
Whereas in the films, he's Doctor Who. I mean, Who is basically his surname.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.200
From the series, I think Ian and Barbara were like two schoolteachers.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:30.000
But with our film, it was Doctor Who and Susan

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:36.490
Barbara were his granddaughters and Ian was the love interest I think and it

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:42.610
made it more sort of family orientated I think it was much much a warmer

00:08:42.610 --> 00:09:00.000
atmosphere Susan dear don't go too far yet the movies of course had a different cast

00:09:00.120 --> 00:09:01.500
to the TV show.

00:09:01.500 --> 00:09:02.800
They couldn't use the television cast

00:09:02.800 --> 00:09:03.800
for a number of reasons.

00:09:03.800 --> 00:09:05.060
The first of which was they were, of course,

00:09:05.060 --> 00:09:08.390
busy 48 weeks of the year making the television series.

00:09:08.390 --> 00:09:10.990
Also, it was important to the producers of the films

00:09:10.990 --> 00:09:14.460
that they had names which could be transatlantic draws.

00:09:14.460 --> 00:09:16.960
And of course, Peter Cushing was very famous in the States

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:19.280
because of his association with the Hammer horror films.

00:09:19.280 --> 00:09:21.730
And so he was an ideal choice for the lead.

00:09:21.730 --> 00:09:23.090
Roy Castle had already appeared

00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:24.490
in previous Amicus productions,

00:09:24.490 --> 00:09:26.580
such as "Doctor Terror's House of Horrors."

00:09:26.580 --> 00:09:27.780
And they knew that he would be an attraction

00:09:27.780 --> 00:09:29.160
for the teenagers as well.

00:09:30.190 --> 00:09:31.750
Heat must have been indescribable.

00:09:32.170 --> 00:09:33.490
It's turned the earth into ashes.

00:09:35.910 --> 00:09:37.230
Oh, well, the soil can't be that bad.

00:09:37.270 --> 00:09:39.030
It still grows these shrubs and trees.

00:09:39.580 --> 00:09:40.440
Look at that.

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:50.530
I remember Gordon Fleming coming down to the stage school that I was at, the Corona,

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:57.310
and seeing a whole bunch of girls that would be the age, the Susan he was looking for.

00:09:57.810 --> 00:10:00.000
And we all had a chat with him.

00:10:00.320 --> 00:10:04.840
And then I got a call a few weeks later to go down to Shepparton Studios

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:07.850
to actually do a film test for the part.

00:10:08.260 --> 00:10:11.900
I had to go into a sort of library room, pick a book off the shelf

00:10:11.900 --> 00:10:13.860
and sit and read it aloud.

00:10:14.400 --> 00:10:17.220
And it was one of Spike Milligan's poetry books,

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:21.350
which was quite, for me, at that age, I didn't understand a word of it.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:24.210
But I think he really enjoyed that.

00:10:24.210 --> 00:10:27.790
And by the time I got home from Shepparton Studios,

00:10:27.990 --> 00:10:30.000
I'd got a call that I'd been cast as soon.

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:37.820
them. I would love to see it now in a cinema and to hear the reaction when you

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:43.880
see you get this kind of John Wayne legs akimbo like that and they come up and

00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.050
then you get what purports to be a chest with no hair in it and then on the top

00:10:48.050 --> 00:10:53.620
of it is Jean Shrimpton. What are you? What do you want? Her name is Alidon. I am

00:10:53.620 --> 00:11:00.000
a thal. Well the thals were, first of all they were possessed of

00:11:00.000 --> 00:11:04.060
of the most beautiful tribe of women, led by Yvonne.

00:11:04.060 --> 00:11:05.260
Were you the kind of queen?

00:11:05.260 --> 00:11:08.440
- I was, I was the queen, yes.

00:11:08.440 --> 00:11:10.900
- Well, I had a tribe of very tall women

00:11:10.900 --> 00:11:13.900
and I don't know why I was cast.

00:11:13.900 --> 00:11:15.740
Because I'm five foot three

00:11:15.740 --> 00:11:18.820
and the tribe were all models.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.500
- It is from the young girl.

00:11:20.500 --> 00:11:23.690
She says that the Dalek people wish to make friends with us.

00:11:23.690 --> 00:11:25.790
Food will be left for us in their great hall.

00:11:25.790 --> 00:11:28.210
We are to collect it tomorrow.

00:11:28.210 --> 00:11:30.000
- I thought they were beautiful, absolutely.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.160
The make-up was wonderful, I'm sure they didn't enjoy doing it.

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:38.540
I'm sure they had to be there at the crack of dawn before I even arrived to get all this make-up on.

00:11:38.880 --> 00:11:43.120
And the wonderful false eyelashes they were wearing, I'm sure the men didn't enjoy that.

00:11:43.200 --> 00:11:49.290
We did actually have a pretty ugly moment on the set when all the guys who were playing the Thals,

00:11:49.290 --> 00:11:55.580
who were really tough, heavy guys, and Geoffrey Toon and I trying to appear more virile and muscular

00:11:55.580 --> 00:12:00.000
than they were, they got the real Covent Garden portrait

00:12:00.000 --> 00:12:03.800
They got the guys who really sling the apples and pears up and down, that kind of thing.

00:12:03.800 --> 00:12:10.620
And they were tough guys, until the moment when they got into makeup, and they started

00:12:10.620 --> 00:12:14.820
putting this grey makeup on, and then they started putting eyebrows on like Gene Shrimpton,

00:12:15.230 --> 00:12:18.150
and then they started putting fingers on like Twiggy.

00:12:18.520 --> 00:12:23.910
Then the makeup ladies said, OK, guys, now, before we make up your bodies, which they

00:12:23.910 --> 00:12:26.410
were all looking forward to, they were looking forward to the makeup girls making up their

00:12:26.410 --> 00:12:30.000
bodies, but before we make up your bodies, we would like you...

00:12:30.000 --> 00:12:34.750
"to shave all the hairs off your arms and your chests."

00:12:34.750 --> 00:12:36.950
And they couldn't believe they'd been asked to,

00:12:36.950 --> 00:12:41.350
because this was absolutely a blow against their masculinity.

00:12:41.350 --> 00:12:43.820
It's like Samson and Delilah.

00:12:43.820 --> 00:12:46.250
And they got more money for it.

00:12:46.250 --> 00:12:48.170
They had a meeting, and they decided

00:12:48.170 --> 00:12:50.530
the only way they'd do it as damage is,

00:12:50.530 --> 00:12:52.940
it's a very useful thing in law, they get more money.

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:54.820
So they gave 'em about another pound a day,

00:12:54.820 --> 00:12:57.420
and they all went and shaved the hair off their chests.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:13:00.000
And they, it changed their personalities.

00:13:00.410 --> 00:13:01.870
they all became a little bit more

00:13:01.870 --> 00:13:03.690
subdued and things, but it was as if they'd given

00:13:03.690 --> 00:13:05.820
something up. They couldn't wait for the day

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:07.540
when they could grow their hair again. So there, you see,

00:13:07.880 --> 00:13:09.500
the moral of that story is every thal

00:13:09.500 --> 00:13:12.180
has his price. So a thal gave

00:13:12.180 --> 00:13:13.980
you this cape, Susan? Yes,

00:13:14.260 --> 00:13:15.960
his name's Allodon. He told

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:17.540
me his people live far from here,

00:13:17.910 --> 00:13:19.470
that this year their crops were ruined,

00:13:19.650 --> 00:13:21.150
and they came here in search of food.

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:23.540
He said his people would gladly give the Daleks

00:13:23.540 --> 00:13:25.460
the formula for the drug, if the Daleks

00:13:25.460 --> 00:13:26.760
would give them food in return.

00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:29.280
My first shot

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:31.590
was with Peter.

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:35.760
And I think it was his first scene in the film too.

00:13:35.760 --> 00:13:38.280
That's when we're both sitting by the fireside

00:13:38.280 --> 00:13:41.300
and we're just talking about what the plan was going to be.

00:13:41.300 --> 00:13:43.820
And just before the take,

00:13:43.820 --> 00:13:46.930
he said, he, this vastly experienced film actor,

00:13:46.930 --> 00:13:49.210
you know, all these credits behind him said,

00:13:49.210 --> 00:13:51.620
"Oh gosh, I always feel so nervous, don't you,

00:13:51.620 --> 00:13:53.300
"on the first scene in the film."

00:13:53.300 --> 00:13:55.450
And because he asked me that, I'd totally forgot my nerves

00:13:55.450 --> 00:13:56.610
and we had a lovely scene

00:13:56.610 --> 00:13:58.570
and I never had a nerve on the film after that.

00:13:58.570 --> 00:14:00.000
That's the kind of guy he was.

00:14:00.000 --> 00:14:03.960
did it quite deliberately to put me at my ease. I thought he was a little too

00:14:03.960 --> 00:14:09.940
gentle. I thought he was too kindly and too warm. The thing that Bill had, Bill

00:14:09.940 --> 00:14:15.050
Hartnell had, was, as I say, this irascibility. He was a bad-tempered old

00:14:15.050 --> 00:14:24.740
curmudgeonly figure and I'd like to see more of that in the character. Oh, well. Peter Cushing was

00:14:24.740 --> 00:14:30.000
a great flower man. He enjoyed the flowers and things and he'd come up with this

00:14:30.000 --> 00:14:37.020
idea about this flower like a tulip that Susan finds in the forest and he

00:14:37.020 --> 00:14:42.290
actually phoned up the Horticultural Society and actually found a name and he

00:14:42.290 --> 00:14:45.890
came to me and said now this is what we'll call it.

00:14:45.890 --> 00:14:49.450
That looks like a tulip.

00:14:49.450 --> 00:14:51.300
It's a Lillium philadelphicum.

00:14:51.300 --> 00:14:55.720
Is it now? It's a Lillium philadelphicum is it?

00:14:55.720 --> 00:15:00.000
I had to try and remember this what I thought was a really long couple of words.

00:15:00.060 --> 00:15:03.780
And when we were filming, I mean, everybody thinks that maybe you've got hundreds of these

00:15:03.780 --> 00:15:11.050
flowers, but we only had three, so we had to get it right.

00:15:11.050 --> 00:15:14.690
And even at that age, I knew that we had to do this.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:17.600
There was no more if we'd used the three up.

00:15:33.160 --> 00:15:36.470
Well, I must say, Roy and I got on really well.

00:15:40.150 --> 00:15:44.890
Well, of course, Roy was always such tremendous fun to work with.

00:15:45.010 --> 00:15:46.710
His little bright, shining eyes.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:47.850
Such a talent.

00:15:48.500 --> 00:15:51.400
And everything everybody's ever said about him.

00:15:51.500 --> 00:15:55.880
Generous, humane, no side, no star.

00:15:56.200 --> 00:15:58.100
I mean, just a wonderful person.

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:00.000
We will send a small party that night.

00:16:00.000 --> 00:16:06.360
way led by our brave friend the rest will come with us to try to find a way to approach the city

00:16:06.360 --> 00:16:19.410
you will go with him through the swamp hey all right i remember that some days he would bring

00:16:19.410 --> 00:16:25.960
his trumpet to the studios with him to practice things but the trouble was he had the dressing

00:16:25.960 --> 00:16:30.000
room down the corridor to me and of course when i wasn't on

00:16:30.000 --> 00:16:37.370
set, I used to have to be doing my schoolwork with my chaperone and tutor, Mrs Nelson. And

00:16:37.370 --> 00:16:43.450
Roy would start playing all these songs and I'd be trying to concentrate on history, on

00:16:43.450 --> 00:16:48.050
things like that. And then you'd get carried away and sort of start singing along with

00:16:48.050 --> 00:16:52.120
some of the tunes that you recognised he was playing. And in the end, some days we'd give

00:16:52.120 --> 00:16:55.720
up and send a message down to him and say, well, can you play this one? We like that one.

00:16:55.940 --> 00:16:58.920
Excuse me, Mr Darlick, would you care to move on to this key?

00:16:59.840 --> 00:16:59.980
Thank you.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:11.360
really was quite magical and I for a time you actually believed you were on

00:17:11.360 --> 00:17:18.840
another planet and they actually built Dalek City far to the other end of the

00:17:18.840 --> 00:17:25.330
studio and we could actually as though we were actually seeing the Dalek City

00:17:25.330 --> 00:17:30.000
in the distance it was all there for us it was very high up and

00:17:30.000 --> 00:17:36.220
seemed very remote. It really did look like something distant and slightly

00:17:36.220 --> 00:17:40.210
frightening and alien. I mean it's a very strange thing on the film set, just that

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:45.770
thing between reality and make-believe. I do remember that scene

00:17:45.770 --> 00:17:48.890
I saw again when we were all rushing down the mountainside. Some people skidded on

00:17:48.890 --> 00:17:53.280
the mountainside and got rather sore bottoms were skidding down it.

00:17:53.280 --> 00:18:00.000
I had to climb down and I think Gordon Fleming

00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:05.100
at the time saw the look of horror on my face because it was quite slippery it was

00:18:05.100 --> 00:18:09.360
made of fiberglass and everything and the camera was to stand at the top and

00:18:09.360 --> 00:18:14.940
pan down with me and I think he saw me looking over the edge and saying and he

00:18:14.940 --> 00:18:19.860
said don't worry I'll come down with you I'll just be behind the camera and I'll

00:18:19.860 --> 00:18:24.930
catch you if you fall so I put my faith in him and said okay all right I'll do it

00:18:42.030 --> 00:18:45.430
I never actually saw anybody in the Daleks.

